Aim: Inter-Process Communication (IPC) using Shared Memory – Create a program that exchanges data between processes using shared memory (shmget(), shmat()).

Theory:
•	IPC using Shared Memory in C
Inter-Process Communication (IPC) allows two or more processes to exchange data.
Shared memory is one of the fastest IPC methods because multiple processes access the same memory area
i)	shmat() - attaches shared memory to the process. 
ii)	shmdt() - detaches shared memory.
iii)	shmget() - creates a shared memory segment.
iv)	shmid → stores the ID of the created shared memory

Code:
#include <stdio.h>
#include <sys/ipc.h>
#include <sys/shm.h>

int main()
{
    int shmid;
    char *str;

    // Create shared memory
    shmid = shmget(1234, 100, 0666 | IPC_CREAT);

    // Attach shared memory
    str = (char *)shmat(shmid, NULL, 0);

    // Write data
    printf("Enter message: ");
    scanf("%s", str);  //For long message (“% [^\n]”, str);

    // Read data
    printf("Message from shared memory: %s\n", str);

    // Detach shared memory
    shmdt(str);

    return 0;
}


